    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "OL09-00-000785 - OL 9 audit logs must be group-owned by root or by a restricted logging group to prevent unauthorized read access."
      info        : "Unauthorized disclosure of audit records can reveal system and configuration data to attackers, thus compromising its confidentiality.

Satisfies: SRG-OS-000057-GPOS-00027, SRG-OS-000058-GPOS-00028, SRG-OS-000059-GPOS-00029, SRG-OS-000206-GPOS-00084"
      solution    : "Configure OL 9 audit logs to be group-owned by \"root\" or a restricted logging group.

Change the group of the directory of \"/var/log/audit\" to be owned by a correct group.

Identify the group that is configured to own audit log:

$ sudo grep -P '^[ ]*log_group[ ]+=.*$' /etc/audit/auditd.conf

Change the ownership to that group:

$ sudo chgrp ${GROUP} /var/log/audit"
