    <custom_item>
      type        : CMD_EXEC
      description : "OL09-00-002392 - OL 9 must disable the ability of systemd to spawn an interactive boot process."
      info        : "Using interactive or recovery boot, the console user could disable auditing, firewalls, or other services, weakening system security."
      solution    : "Configure OL 9 to allocate sufficient audit_backlog_limit to disable the ability of systemd to spawn an interactive boot process with the following command:

$ sudo grubby --update-kernel=ALL --remove-args=\"systemd.confirm_spawn\""
      reference   : "800-171|3.4.2,800-171r3|03.04.02a.,800-53|CM-6b.,800-53r5|CM-6b.,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-000366,CN-L3|8.1.10.6(d),CSF|PR.IP-1,CSF2.0|DE.CM-09,CSF2.0|PR.PS-01,DISA_Benchmark|Oracle_Linux_9_STIG,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ISO-27001-2022|A.8.9,ITSG-33|CM-6b.,NESA|T3.2.1,Rule-ID|SV-271736r1091920_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002392,SWIFT-CSCv1|2.3,Vuln-ID|V-271736"
      see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Oracle_Linux_9_V1R6_STIG.zip"
      cmd         : "(/sbin/grubby --info=ALL | /bin/grep args | /bin/grep 'systemd.confirm_spawn') 2>&1 | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\" ; else print \"fail\"}'"
      expect      : "^pass$"
    </custom_item>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "Check /etc/default/grub for vsyscall=none"
          file        : "/etc/default/grub"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="
          expect      : "^[^#]+\\bvsyscall=none\\b"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "grubby"
          cmd         : "(/sbin/grubby --info=ALL | /bin/grep args | /bin/grep -v 'vsyscall=none') 2>&1 | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\" ; else print \"fail\"}'"
          expect      : "^pass$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "OL09-00-002393 - OL 9 must disable virtual system calls."
          info        : "System calls are special routines in the Linux kernel, which userspace applications ask to do privileged tasks. Invoking a system call is an expensive operation because the processor must interrupt the currently executing task and switch context to kernel mode and then back to userspace after the system call completes. Virtual system calls map into user space a page that contains some variables and the implementation of some system calls. This allows the system calls to be executed in userspace to alleviate the context switching expense.

Virtual system calls provide an opportunity of attack for a user who has control of the return instruction pointer. Disabling virtual system calls help to prevent return-oriented programming (ROP) attacks via buffer overflows and overruns. If the system intends to run containers based on OL 6 components, virtual system calls will have to be enabled so the components function properly."
          solution    : "Document the use of virtual system calls with the ISSO as an operational requirement or disable them with the following command:

$ sudo grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args=\"vsyscall=none\"

Add or modify the following line in \"/etc/default/grub\" to ensure the configuration survives kernel updates:

GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX=\"vsyscall=none\""
          reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001084,DISA_Benchmark|Oracle_Linux_9_STIG,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-271737r1094967_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002393,Vuln-ID|V-271737"
          see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Oracle_Linux_9_V1R6_STIG.zip"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

    <if>
      <condition auto:"FAILED" type:"AND">
        <custom_item>
          type        : FILE_CONTENT_CHECK
          description : "Check that virtual system calls are disabled by default to persist in kernel updates"
          file        : "/etc/default/grub"
          regex       : "^[\\s]*GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX="
          expect      : "^[^#]+\\bpage_poison=1\\b"
        </custom_item>

        <custom_item>
          type        : CMD_EXEC
          description : "Check that the current GRUB 2 configuration has page poisoning enabled"
          cmd         : "(/sbin/grubby --info=ALL | /bin/grep args | /bin/grep -v 'page_poison=1') 2>&1 | /bin/awk '{print} END {if (NR == 0) print \"pass\" ; else print \"fail\"}'"
          expect      : "^pass$"
        </custom_item>
      </condition>

      <then>
        <report type:"PASSED">
          description : "OL09-00-002394 - OL 9 must clear the page allocator to prevent use-after-free attacks."
          info        : "Poisoning writes an arbitrary value to freed pages, so any modification or reference to that page after being freed or before being initialized will be detected and prevented. This prevents many types of use-after-free vulnerabilities at little performance cost. Also prevents leak of data and detection of corrupted memory."
          solution    : "Configure OL 9 to enable page poisoning with the following commands:

$ sudo grubby --update-kernel=ALL --args=\"page_poison=1\"

Add or modify the following line in \"/etc/default/grub\" to ensure the configuration survives kernel updates:

GRUB_CMDLINE_LINUX=\"page_poison=1\""
          reference   : "800-53|SC-3,800-53r5|SC-3,CAT|II,CCI|CCI-001084,DISA_Benchmark|Oracle_Linux_9_STIG,GDPR|32.1.b,HIPAA|164.306(a)(1),ITSG-33|SC-3,ITSG-33|SC-3a.,NESA|T3.4.1,NESA|T4.3.1,NESA|T4.3.2,Rule-ID|SV-271738r1092600_rule,STIG-ID|OL09-00-002394,Vuln-ID|V-271738"
          see_also    : "https://dl.dod.cyber.mil/wp-content/uploads/stigs/zip/U_Oracle_Linux_9_V1R6_STIG.zip"
          show_output : YES
        </report>
      </then>
    </if>

